Missing Number:
public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
    int xor = nums.length;

    for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
        xor ^= i;
        xor ^= nums[i];
    }

    return xor;
}

Sum of Two Integers Without +
public int getSum(int a, int b) {
    while(b != 0){
        int carry = (a & b) << 1;
        a = a ^ b;
        b = carry;
    }
    return a;
}

| Operation                    | Formula                            | Explanation                                                                                            |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Check kth bit                | (n & (1<<k)) != 0                  | 1<<k creates a mask where only kth bit is 1. AND operation checks whether kth bit in n is set.   |
| Set kth bit                  | n | (1<<k)                       | OR with a mask sets kth bit to 1 without affecting other bits.                                       |
| Clear kth bit                | n & ~(1<<k)                      | 1<<k creates mask, ~ flips it so kth bit becomes 0 and others 1. AND clears only kth bit.      |
| Toggle kth bit               | n ^ (1<<k)                       | XOR flips the kth bit. If it was 0 it becomes 1, if 1 it becomes 0.                            |
| Remove lowest set bit        | n & (n-1)                        | Subtracting 1 flips all bits after lowest set bit. AND removes the rightmost 1.                      |
| Check if power of 2          | (n & (n-1)) == 0                 | Powers of 2 contain only one set bit. Removing lowest set bit makes number 0.                        |
| Extract lowest set bit       | n & -n                           | Keeps only the rightmost set bit because -n in two’s complement preserves lowest set bit during AND. |
| Multiply by 2^k              | n << k                           | Left shift moves bits left by k positions, equivalent to multiplying by 2^k.                       |
| Divide by 2^k                | n >> k                           | Right shift moves bits right by k positions, equivalent to integer division by 2^k.                |
| Check odd/even               | (n & 1)                          | Last bit determines parity. 1 means odd, 0 means even.                                             |
| Count set bits               | while(n){ n &= (n-1); count++; } | Each iteration removes one set bit, so loop runs only for number of 1s.                              |
| Turn off lowest set bit      | n & (n-1)                        | Same trick as removing rightmost set bit.                                                              |
| Turn on lowest unset bit     | n \| (n+1)                       | Adding 1 changes first 0 from right into 1; OR keeps it set.                                       |
| Check opposite signs         | (a ^ b) < 0                      | Sign bit differs for opposite signs, XOR makes sign bit 1.                                           |
| Swap without temp            | a^=b; b^=a; a^=b;                | XOR reversibility swaps values without extra memory.                                                   |
| Get binary kth bit value     | (n >> k) & 1                     | Shift kth bit to last position, then isolate using & 1.                                              |
| Create mask of k bits        | (1<<k)-1                         | Example: k=4 → 1111. Useful for extracting lower bits.                                             |
| Clear all bits from LSB to k | n & (~((1<<(k+1))-1))            | Creates mask with lower bits 0, higher bits 1.                                                     |
| Keep only lowest k bits      | n & ((1<<k)-1)                   | Mask keeps lower k bits and removes remaining higher bits.                                             |
